# Eliminating an `Option`.
#
# This file is the answer to §14F.2a, which the type checker recorded in
# the implementer's words as "no program can read a map and use the
# result". §5.4 makes indexing bounds-checked, so `table at name` is an
# `Option of Whole`; `when` eliminates one, but `when` is a statement, and a
# sort key is an expression.
#
# `valueOr` is that elimination in expression position, and nothing about
# it is special: it is an ordinary ZDeceptron function over an ordinary
# `when`, which is exactly §14F.2's claim about what the library should be.
# The alternative §14F.2a floated — making `when` itself an expression —
# would have been a grammar change, and it would have made every arm of
# every `when` a value even where the arms produce view nodes.

function valueOr with maybe, fallback
    when maybe
        Some with value
            give value
        None
            give fallback

function isSome of maybe
    when maybe
        Some with value
            give yes
        None
            give no

function isNone of maybe
    give not (isSome of maybe)

# `Option of Option of T` flattened to `Option of T`.
#
# The other half of #103. `map each x in maybe to (lookup of x)` gives an
# `Option of Option of T` when the body is itself a lookup, and this is
# what makes the pair of them `andThen`:
#
#   flattenOption of (map each id in chosen to (userAt of id))
#
# There is no `andThen` function beside it, and there could not be: it
# would have to take the transform as a value, and this language has no
# value that is one. What it has instead is a clause that takes the
# transform as *syntax*, and this five-line function is everything the
# clause does not do.
function flattenOption of maybe
    when maybe
        Some with value
            give value
        None
            give None
